Z||000004||theogre||09-06-2000||12:14 PM||||Nearly any FWD that will fit in the space can be moved to Fiero. You don't even have to stick with GM product. Chrysler has some amazingly strong V6 setups. There are a bunch of turbo charged 4 cyls from GM and others.<P>There are 3 areas to the conversion.<P>1. physically mounting it to the Fiero cradle. Mostly annoying and messy.<P>2. Axles. There are only a few CV joint types used in U.S. cars. The main issue is how long they are. According to what I'm told CV's unlimited can assemble almost any custom axle you want.<P>3. electrical. This is the hardest part for allot of people. You can do trial and error fitting on #'s 1 & 2 but you can easily fry ECM's and start fires if you do that with the engine electrical systems.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ramzy_steele||09-06-2000||12:24 PM||||Thanks the ogre.  So you saying that It will be relatively strait forward but I may run into prblems with electrical and the axle?  I could be wrong and probalyl am but the tranny and axle look like they are together soa re you saying front wheel drive cars are not the same?  Or will the trans-axle not fit the actual rrods turning my wheels?  ( Sorry for lack of knwledge as to what everything is called)  If the tranny wont fit he wheel turning things then could I just take out the whole fornt wheel setup and brin it back to the back along with brakes and everything?  Thanks ALot||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||09-06-2000||12:33 PM||||It's a pretty generally statement, but basically, no, it's wrong.<P>The Fiero rear cradle is *based* on the GM FWD configuration but matches none of the FWD cars.<P>People like to say "It's the Citation front end". It's not, plain and simple. Similar? Yes, it's similar.||||reg||13
