Z||000008||ramzy_steele||09-06-2000||01:01 PM||||I forgot to mention if the front wheel drive cars wotn work then how do they do the caddilac conversions?||||reg||1
Z||000009||ThatRickGuy||09-06-2000||02:03 PM||||The front wheel drive cars do work, but it's a matter of money to make them. the 3.1, 3.4, 3400, 3.8, 3800, N* and 350 are all out of front engine cars, and all but the 350 (I think the N* is FWD, not sure on that one tho, never had the money to look at a cadilac) are front wheel drive. If you're looking for a cheep swap, like arround $1000, you may find something, but I doubt you'll be happy, it'll be more work and money in the long run. But if your 2.8 is still running, you can get it rebuilt for just under a grand, or even change it into a 3.1 for afew $100 more (pistons and crank). my bill keeps going up tho because of little things, I'm right arround $1700 on my 3.2 convertion of my 2.8 block. It started out at $1200, but my mechanic couldn't find a reconditioned 3.1 crank ($50 vs $300) and then I found out my rods are basically all fried, if the can be reconditioned it's $120, but I may need to chip in more for a new one. ahh well. search the forums, lots of people have tried lots of things, just take a look.||||reg||1
Z||000010||V8Fiero||09-06-2000||10:59 PM||||I just hate to hear those two words in the same sentence. Cheap and Fast. <P>Im not knocking anyone, I just learned from experiance that cheaper isnt always better. If you really want to swap engines, I would do three things.<P>1. Research<P>2. Research<P>3. Research<P>I think I made my point. I installed a V8 thinking all I would have to pay for was the kit and the rest was going to be a piece of cake. Well, unless you spend 3g's (by no means cheap) for the entire kit, not including the engine, you are still going to need additional things to complete the install. <P>Just some things to think about I suppose. I dont think there is such a thing as a cheap install, one that will perform and last anyway.<P>Thanks for the space.<BR>Scooter<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||stimpy||09-06-2000||11:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ramzy_steele:<BR>Can anyone else verify the front wheel drives not working?<B>  I would liek to note I have all the time in the world.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Don't take offence, but are you in prison or something?  You might try using a carved bar of soap coated with shoe polish, a sharpened spoon and some paper mache to fabricate the parts you'll need to put the warden's Camry powerplant in the Fiero. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||ur2biteme||09-07-2000||02:56 AM||||dammit stimpy!  am i the only one that gets a good laugh out of your comments?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>165k and a bad fender :(||||reg||10
Z||000013||Jonathan||09-07-2000||08:40 AM||||You may want to check out Ed Park's Fiero Factory.  <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html</A> <P>He has info on the 3.4 Camaro/Firebird swap.  It is a very straight forward install.  Yes there are a few details to work out, but most of that is listed on his site.  I was recently at the Fiero Factory, and asked if he had a working one around.  He pointed me to the fence and said "take it for a drive."  I looked at it first. Very plain!  No special exhaust(Think really small tips, stock slow look, etc)  I looked at the engine, couldn't tell the difference.  Then I drove it.  I was sold!  He told me he kept it looking plain so that he could really have fun winning the stoplight races etc.<P>I had a V6 Firebird, but that engine really comes alive in the Fiero.  Now I am looking to finish my bodywork, and that is the swap I will be performing!<P>There are a lot of swaps out there.  Do your research, and if possible, drive them.  It helps your perspective!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||JohnnyK||09-07-2000||10:46 AM||||I don't understand moving the starter to the other side? The start mounts right to the engine... Obviously the engine comes with the holes in the right spot?||||reg||1
Z||000015||cowans||09-07-2000||01:58 PM||||To answer "Johnny", the starter must be moved to the opposite side to allow for your right axle to exit the tranny.... which comes to the next move, installing a remote oil filter  because the starter just displaced the oil filter space. And yes, conversions are not cheap. Good building experience, Sandy||||reg||12
